-agent }\n33\t  52  spec:\n34\t  53    replicas: 3\n35\t  54    selector:\n36\t  55      matchLabels: { app: clinic-agent }\n37\t  56    template:\n38\t  57      metadata:\n39\t  58        labels: { app: clinic-agent }\n40\t  59      spec:\n41\t  60>       serviceAccountName: clinic-agent\n42\t  61        automountServiceAccountToken: false\n43\t  62        hostUsers: false\n44\t  63        hostNetwork: false\n45\t  64        hostPID: false\n46\t  65        hostIPC: false\n47\t  66        enableServiceLinks: false\n48\t  67        securityContext:\n49\t  68          runAsNonRoot: true\n50\t  69          runAsUser: 65532\n51\t  70          runAsGroup: 65532\n52\t  71          fsGroup: 65532\n53\t  72          seccompProfile:\n54\t```\n55\t\n56\t## A01.2\n57\t\n58\t**Check:** Tool and API clients get credentials from the agent's own principal, not from the launching user's forwarded token or session cookie.\n59\t\n60\t**How to verify:** 1) Find every place tool and API clients get a token or key. 2) Trace where each comes from: workload identity or token exchange, versus the incoming request's `Authorization` header or session cookie. 3) Check every client, not just the main one. 4) Answer no if any client forwards the user's token or cookie as-is, or uses a shared static key.\n61\t\n62\t**Reviewer's evidence:** src/integrations/eligibility-a2a.ts:56 — \"const token = await exchangeToken(opts.workloadToken, req.clinicId, req.patientId);\"\n63\t\n64\t**Reviewer's note:** Peer calls use a token exchanged from the workload token; EHR, payments and SMS use per-clinic service credentials from Secrets Manager\n65\t\n66\t`src/integrations/eligibility-a2a.ts` lines 44–68 (cited lines marked >):\n67\t```\n68\t  44        scope: `eligibility:read clinic:${clinicId} patient:${patientId}`,\n69\t  45        requested_token_type: \"urn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:access_token\",\n70\t  46      }),\n71\t  47    });\n72\t  48    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`token exchange failed: ${res.status}`);\n73\t  49    return ((await res.json()) as { access_token: string }).access_token;\n74\t  50  }\n75\t  51  \n76\t  52  export async function checkEligibility(\n77\t  53    req: { clinicId: string; patientId: string; payerId?: string; serviceCode: string; dateOfService: string },\n78\t  54    opts: { workloadToken: string; traceparent: string; signal: AbortSignal },\n79\t  55  ): Promise<Eligibility> {\n80\t  56>   const token = await exchangeToken(opts.workloadToken, req.clinicId, req.patientId);\n81\t  57    const requestId = randomUUID();\n82\t  58    const res = await fetch(env.eligibilityUrl, {\n83\t  59      method: \"POST\",\n84\t  60      signal: opts.signal,\n85\t  61      headers: { authorization: `Bearer ${token}`, \"content-type\": \"application/json\", traceparent: opts.traceparent },\n86\t  62      body: JSON.stringify({\n87\t  63        jsonrpc: \"2.0\",\n88\t  64        id: requestId,\n89\t  65        method: \"message/send\",\n90\t  66        params: {\n91\t  67          message: {\n92\t  68            role: \"user\",\n93\t```\n94\t\n95\t## A01.3\n96\t\n97\t**Check:** When a user starts a run, the user is carried as a delegated subject (for example a token exchange with separate act and sub), while the agent keeps its own principal.\n98\t\n99\t**How to verify:** 1) Find the code that starts a run for a user. 2) Look for token exchange (RFC 8693, `subject_token`, `actor_token`, `act`/`sub` claims) or a separate delegated-subject field. 3) Check the agent's own principal is still the actor. 4) Answer no if the user's token is used directly or the user replaces the agent identity. Answer unknown if the exchange happens in an outside identity service.\n100\t\n101\t**Reviewer's evidence:** src/agent/identity.ts:70 — \"subject: Subject;\"\n102\t\n103\t**Reviewer's note:** RunContext carries the patient or staff user as a separate subject while the agent's own identity() fields are unchanged\n104\t\n105\t`src/agent/identity.ts` lines 58–82 (